ch structure, different observations mean different nodes, so I'm sticking with A-FDT and its submit recommendation.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm applying A-FDT as the essay sets it out: two identical copies seeing different inputs, with each copy's decision node tied to its own observation. Under that framing I intervene only on my own node and treat the other copy's action as a sibling held at its prior, unless an edge rule says its mechanism is computing my output. Since it isn't, the other copy's choice is fixed for my purposes, and defecting comes out dominant in the prisoner's dilemma.\n\nUnder those same assumptions, the other copy's action is fixed for my purposes, and I'm weighing whether a subjunctive-dependence argument could make the two nodes the same function, given symmetric inputs. Following the essay's switch structure, different observations mean different nodes, so I'm sticking with A-FDT and its submit recommendation.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10478,"completion_tokens":760,"total_tokens":11238,"cost":0.057112,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.057112,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.041912,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.0152},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":412,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}